JPA is a **specification** in `jakarta.persistence` — a portable API. Hibernate is one of its **implementations** that adds non-standard features (second-level cache, `@Formula`, criteria extensions). Code that uses only JPA types is portable; code that imports `org.hibernate.*` is locked to Hibernate.

```java
// ---------- 100% JPA (portable to EclipseLink, OpenJPA, DataNucleus) ----------
@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
public class User {
    @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY) private Long id;
    @Column(nullable = false, unique = true) private String email;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "user", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<Order> orders = new ArrayList<>();
}

@Repository
public class UserDao {
    @PersistenceContext private EntityManager em;      // JPA API
    public List<User> findByDomain(String domain) {
        return em.createQuery(
            "SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.email LIKE :d", User.class)   // JPQL (portable)
            .setParameter("d", "%@" + domain).getResultList();
    }
}

// ---------- Hibernate-only (not portable) ----------
@Entity
@org.hibernate.annotations.Cache(usage = CacheConcurrencyStrategy.READ_WRITE)  // Hibernate
@org.hibernate.annotations.DynamicUpdate                                        // Hibernate
public class Product {
    @Id private Long id;

    @org.hibernate.annotations.Formula("(SELECT AVG(r.stars) FROM reviews r WHERE r.product_id = id)")
    private Double avgRating;   // computed column — Hibernate extension
}

// Dropping to the native Hibernate Session:
@Autowired private EntityManager em;
Session session = em.unwrap(Session.class);
session.enableFilter("active").setParameter("flag", true);   // Hibernate filters
```

- Every Spring Data JPA project transitively uses **Hibernate** as the provider unless explicitly switched. You are already on Hibernate — the question is whether your code *depends* on its extensions.
- Stay in `jakarta.persistence.*` for 95% of code; reach for `org.hibernate.*` only when you need features JPA does not define (multi-tenancy filters, `@Formula`, `@Any`).
- JPA's `EntityManager` is a thin wrapper — `em.unwrap(Session.class)` gives you the Hibernate `Session` for advanced use without rewriting repositories.

**SINGLE_TABLE:**

```java
@Entity
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.SINGLE_TABLE)
@DiscriminatorColumn(name = "vehicle_type", discriminatorType = DiscriminatorType.STRING)
public abstract class Vehicle {
    @Id @GeneratedValue Long id;
    String vin;
}

@Entity @DiscriminatorValue("CAR")
public class Car extends Vehicle {
    Integer numberOfDoors;
}

@Entity @DiscriminatorValue("TRUCK")
public class Truck extends Vehicle {
    BigDecimal payloadCapacity;
}
```

**JOINED:**

```java
@Entity
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.JOINED)
public abstract class Vehicle {
    @Id @GeneratedValue Long id;
    @Column(nullable = false) String vin;
}

@Entity
@PrimaryKeyJoinColumn(name = "vehicle_id")
public class Car extends Vehicle {
    @Column(nullable = false) Integer numberOfDoors;
}
```

**TABLE_PER_CLASS:**

```java
@Entity
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.TABLE_PER_CLASS)
public abstract class Vehicle {
    @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.TABLE) Long id;
    String vin;
}
```

**`@MappedSuperclass`:**

```java
@MappedSuperclass
public abstract class Auditable {
    @CreatedDate Instant createdAt;
    @LastModifiedDate Instant updatedAt;
}

@Entity
public class Order extends Auditable {
    @Id @GeneratedValue Long id;
}
```
